set_owner() (lib/IO/K8s/Role/APIObject.pm:881-897) builds an OwnerReference
that the API server rejects, and does it quietly. Three separate defects,
all measured on 4887f658:

  my $dep = $k8s->struct_to_object('Deployment', { metadata => { name => 'web' } });
  my $pod = $k8s->struct_to_object('Pod',        { metadata => { name => 'p' } });
  $pod->set_owner($dep);
  $pod->set_owner($dep);
  $pod->TO_JSON

  "ownerReferences" : [
     { "apiVersion":"apps/v1", "kind":"Deployment", "name":"web",
       "controller":true, "uid":"" },
     { "apiVersion":"apps/v1", "kind":"Deployment", "name":"web",
       "controller":true, "uid":"" }
  ]

1. uid => $owner->metadata->uid // ''
   An empty uid. OwnerReference declares uid as a required Str, so '' passes
   the type check and fails at the cluster -- metadata.ownerReferences.uid
   must not be empty. A locally built owner NEVER has a uid; you only get one
   back from the server. So the common path -- build both objects, link them,
   apply -- is exactly the path that produces the invalid reference. The
   `// ''` is what turns "I cannot know this" into "here is a value".

2. controller => 1, hardcoded.
   Kubernetes permits at most one ownerReference with controller: true. The
   POD says "Add an ownerReference" -- plural-capable, and the method does
   append -- but every one it adds claims to be the controller, so two calls
   produce an object the API server refuses. There is no way to add a
   non-controller owner, which is the ordinary case for anything but the
   single controlling parent.

3. No duplicate check: the same owner twice yields two identical entries.

4. A cryptic death when the owner has no metadata:
     $pod->set_owner($k8s->struct_to_object('Deployment', {}));
     # Can't call method "name" on an undefined value at
     #   lib/IO/K8s/Role/APIObject.pm line 887.
   Neither method nor argument named. Same diagnostic gap as karr #42, in a
   different place. Note the asymmetry: is_owned_by() (:907) carefully
   guards with `$owner->metadata ? ... : undef` and even falls back to
   name+kind when uid is missing -- so the read side already knows uid may
   be absent and metadata may be undef. Only the write side pretends
   otherwise.

Test coverage today is one lives_ok in t/54_single_segment_kind.t:130,
which exists to check that a single-segment owner's kind is not undef. The
value written is asserted nowhere.

WHAT TO DO -- decide the contract before touching the code
  * uid: refuse rather than invent. Either die naming the owner ("cannot
    reference <Kind>/<name>: owner has no uid; the uid is assigned by the
    API server"), or omit the field and let OwnerReference's required
    constraint fail loudly. Do NOT keep writing ''. Which of the two is the
    house answer follows karr #37/#39: fail closed, name what failed.
  * controller: make it a parameter (default open -- decide whether the
    default stays 1 for back-compat and is documented, or flips to 0 with a
    Changes note). At minimum, refuse to add a second controller:true ref.
  * duplicates: same owner twice should be a no-op or an error, not two
    entries.
  * missing metadata: guard and die with the object named, the way
    is_owned_by already guards.
  * blockOwnerDeletion is never set; check whether it should be (upstream
    defaults it false, so leaving it out is fine -- confirm and say so).

Every one of these is a behaviour change on a public method. Changes entry
must state the changed failure mode per the house protocol.

Tests: a new t/ file, or extend t/54. Assert the emitted ownerReferences
structure, both directions, plus the two error messages. The existing
lives_ok must keep passing -- karr #38's claim rides on it.

